\section{Anomalies}{

Weather anomalies around each sample date.

From the catchment daily Daymet series: the climatology of each catchment
is a three-harmonic seasonal cycle fitted to \code{baseline_years}, and the
anomalies are
\itemize{
\item \code{t_anom7}, \code{t_anom30}: mean daily temperature anomaly (C) over the 7 and
30 days ending on the date;
\item \code{t_anom7_dt}, \code{t_anom30_dt}: the same after removing the catchment's
linear trend (pure weather; the raw versions carry the warming trend);
\item \code{p_anom7}, \code{p_anom30}: log(1 + precipitation total, mm) over the window
minus its climatology.
With \code{melt = TRUE}, also \code{melt_doy}, \code{melt_anom} and
\code{days_since_melt} (sample day of year minus melt day).
}

Daymet years have 365 days (31 December is dropped in leap years), so day
of year matches the calendar for every sample date. \code{weather} must include
the baseline years.

Uncertainty (at sample dates): \verb{*_se} is the
point-sampling SE of each anomaly. The same anomalies are computed for
every Daymet pixel behind the catchment (each against its own
climatology), and the SE is their between-pixel SD / sqrt(n) x sqrt(1 -
n / N). For temperature this is exact: the harmonic fit, the trend and the
window means are linear, so the catchment anomaly is the mean of the pixel
anomalies. It is much smaller than the daily SE in \code{weather}, because a
pixel that is warmer than the catchment (higher, lower, south-facing) is
warmer in its climatology too, and that cancels. For precipitation (log
of window totals) it is an approximation. \code{weather_flag}: \code{few_pixels}
where fewer than 3 pixels make the mean, so the SE is unreliable.
Daymet's own interpolation error is not included: it is largely
persistent, so it mostly cancels in anomalies too (see
\code{vignette("weather-daymet")}).
}

\section{Melt timing (\code{melt = TRUE})}{

Snowmelt timing from Daymet snow water equivalent.

For each Daymet pixel behind a catchment, the melt day of each year is the
first day after peak SWE (January to day \code{max_doy}) on which SWE falls
below \code{swe_min} kg/m2; the catchment value is the median over its pixels.
Pixels that never melt (glacier, perennial snow) or never hold snow drop
out of the median. \code{melt_anom} is the departure from the catchment's mean
over \code{baseline_years}.
}

\section{Antecedent summaries}{

Snowfall, summer temperature and degree days for each sample.

From the catchment daily Daymet series, per observation:
\itemize{
\item \code{snowfall_swe_mm}, \code{snowfall_depth_cm}: total over the snow year (from
\code{snow_year_start} month, labelled by the year it ends). Daymet gives
total precipitation, so it is split into snow with a linear ramp (all
snow below \code{t_snow}, all rain above \code{t_rain}) and converted to depth
with Hedstrom and Pomeroy (1998) new-snow density, 67.92 + 51.25 exp(T /
2.59) kg/m3, limited to 50-200. Partial snow years are dropped.
\item \code{summer_tmean_c}: mean temperature in \code{summer_months} of the calendar
year.
\item degree days from 1 January to the sample date: \verb{gdd_base<b>} for each
base, \code{tdd} (thawing, base 0) and \code{fdd} (freezing, negative).
\item with \code{snowoff} (from \code{\link[=ak_snowoff]{ak_snowoff()}}): \code{snowoff_doy}, \code{snowoff_source},
\code{tdd_snowoff} (thawing degree days from snow-off to the sample, 0 if
sampled before snow-off), \code{tdd_snowoff_se} and \code{days_since_snowoff}.
